#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:178
msgid "Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:180
msgid ""
"When a type expression is evaluated at runtime, the resulting value is a "
"*type form* object. This value encodes the information supplied in the type "
"expression, and it represents the type described by that type expression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:184
msgid ""
"``TypeForm`` is a special form that, when used in a type expression, "
"describes a set of type form objects. It accepts a single type argument, "
"which must be a valid type expression. ``TypeForm[T]`` describes the set of "
"all type form objects that represent the type ``T`` or types that are :term:"
"`assignable to <typing:assignable>` ``T``. For example, ``TypeForm[str | "
"None]`` describes the set of all type form objects that represent a type "
"assignable to ``str | None``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:203
msgid ""
"By this same definition, ``TypeForm[Any]`` describes a type form object that "
"represents the type ``Any`` or any type that is assignable to ``Any``. Since "
"all types in the Python type system are assignable to ``Any``, "
"``TypeForm[Any]`` describes the set of all type form objects evaluated from "
"all valid type expressions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:209
msgid ""
"The type expression ``TypeForm``, with no type argument provided, is "
"equivalent to ``TypeForm[Any]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:214
msgid "Implicit ``TypeForm`` Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:216
msgid ""
"When a static type checker encounters an expression that follows all of the "
"syntactic, semantic and contextual rules for a type expression as detailed "
"in the typing spec, the evaluated type of this expression should be "
"assignable to ``TypeForm[T]`` if the type it describes is assignable to "
"``T``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:221
msgid ""
"For example, if a static type checker encounters the expression ``str | "
"None``, it may normally evaluate its type as ``UnionType`` because it "
"produces a runtime value that is an instance of ``types.UnionType``. "
"However, because this expression is a valid type expression, it is also "
"assignable to the type ``TypeForm[str | None]``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:233
msgid ""
"The ``Annotated`` special form is allowed in type expressions, so it can "
"also appear in an expression that is assignable to ``TypeForm``. Consistent "
"with the typing spec's rules for ``Annotated``, a static type checker may "
"choose to ignore any ``Annotated`` metadata that it does not understand::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:241
msgid ""
"A string literal expression containing a valid type expression should "
"likewise be assignable to ``TypeForm``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:246
msgid ""
"Expressions that violate one or more of the syntactic, semantic, or "
"contextual rules for type expressions should not evaluate to a ``TypeForm`` "
"type. The rules for type expression validity are explained in detail within "
"the typing spec, so they are not repeated here::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:267
msgid "Explicit ``TypeForm`` Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:269
msgid ""
"``TypeForm`` also acts as a function that can be called with a single "
"argument. Type checkers should validate that this argument is a valid type "
"expression::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:280
msgid ""
"At runtime the ``TypeForm(...)`` callable simply returns the value passed to "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:282
msgid ""
"This explicit syntax serves two purposes. First, it documents the "
"developer's intent to use the value as a type form object. Second, static "
"type checkers validate that all rules for type expressions are followed::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:292
msgid "Assignability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:294
msgid ""
"``TypeForm`` has a single type parameter, which is covariant. That means "
"``TypeForm[B]`` is assignable to ``TypeForm[A]`` if ``B`` is assignable to "
"``A``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:303
msgid ""
"``type[T]`` is a subtype of ``TypeForm[T]``, which means that ``type[B]`` is "
"assignable to ``TypeForm[A]`` if ``B`` is assignable to ``A``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:311
msgid ""
"``TypeForm`` is a subtype of ``object`` and is assumed to have all of the "
"attributes and methods of ``object``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:316
msgid "Backward Compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:318
msgid ""
"This PEP clarifies static type checker behaviors when evaluating type "
"expressions in \"value expression\" contexts (that is, contexts where type "
"expressions are not mandated by the typing spec). In the absence of a "
"``TypeForm`` type annotation, existing type evaluation behaviors persist, so "
"no backward compatibility issues are anticipated. For example, if a static "
"type checker previously evaluated the type of expression ``str | None`` as "
"``UnionType``, it will continue to do so unless this expression is assigned "
"to a variable or parameter whose type is annotated as ``TypeForm``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:329
msgid "How to Teach This"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:331
msgid ""
"Type expressions are used in annotations to describe which values are "
"accepted by a function parameter, returned by a function, or stored in a "
"variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:347
msgid ""
"Type expressions evaluate to valid *type form* objects at runtime and can be "
"assigned to variables and manipulated like any other data in a program:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:360
msgid ""
"``TypeForm[]`` is how you spell the type of a *type form* object, which is a "
"runtime representation of a type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:363
msgid ""
"``TypeForm`` is similar to ``type``, but ``type`` is compatible only with "
"**class objects** like ``int``, ``str``, ``list``, or ``MyClass``. "
"``TypeForm`` accommodates any type form that can be expressed using a valid "
"type expression, including those with brackets (``list[int]``), union "
"operators (``int | None``), and special forms (``Any``, ``LiteralString``, "
"``Never``, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:370
msgid ""
"Most programmers will not define their *own* functions that accept a "
"``TypeForm`` parameter or return a ``TypeForm`` value. It is more common to "
"pass a type form object to a library function that knows how to decode and "
"use such objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0747.rst:374
msgid ""
"For example, the ``isassignable`` function in the ``trycast`` library can be "
"used like Python's built-in ``isinstance`` function to check whether a value "
"matches the shape of a particular type. ``isassignable`` accepts *any* type "
"form object as input."
msgstr ""

